---
import Code from "#/components/Code.astro";
import Docs from "#/layouts/Docs.astro";
---

<Docs
	page="editors"
	title="Editors"
	lede="Format on save: squill as your editor's SQL formatter, and for the SQL inside your other code."
>
	<section>
		<h2>How it works</h2>
		<p>
			Editors hand a formatter the buffer on stdin and read the result back from
			stdout. Tell squill which file the buffer is with{" "}
			<code>--stdin-filepath</code>, and it formats it exactly as{" "}
			<code>squill fmt</code> would format that file on disk: the nearest{" "}
			<code>squill.toml</code> applies, and so do its{" "}
			<a href="docs/configuration/#rules">rules</a>, so a Rust buffer gets its
			embedded SQL formatted and a <code>*.sql.sqlite</code> file its dialect.
			The file doesn&rsquo;t need to exist yet.
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="sh"
			code={`squill fmt --stdin-filepath src/db.rs < src/db.rs`}
		/>
		<p>
			A buffer squill wouldn&rsquo;t format as a file comes back unchanged: an{" "}
			<code>ignore</code>d or <a href="docs/frozen/">frozen</a> path, or a
			host-language file no <code>[[embedded]]</code> rule covers. So it is safe
			to turn on for every file of a language, in every project.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Helix</h2>
		<p>
			In <code>languages.toml</code>, set squill as the SQL formatter.{" "}
			<code>%&lbrace;buffer_name&rbrace;</code> passes the buffer&rsquo;s path:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="toml"
			file="languages.toml"
			code={`[[language]]
name = "sql"
formatter = { command = "squill", args = ["fmt", "--stdin-filepath", "%{buffer_name}"] }
auto-format = true`}
		/>
		<p>
			A Helix formatter replaces the language server&rsquo;s, and runs alone.
			For SQL embedded in another language, pipe that language&rsquo;s own
			formatter into squill:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="toml"
			file="languages.toml"
			code={`[[language]]
name = "rust"
formatter = { command = "sh", args = ["-c", "rustfmt | squill fmt --stdin-filepath \\"$0\\"", "%{buffer_name}"] }`}
		/>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Language server</h2>
		<p>
			<code>squill language-server start</code> runs a language server on stdin
			and stdout. It formats documents the same way (by their path, config and
			rules included), and it reports what squill leaves alone as warnings where
			they are: statements it passed through verbatim, and embedded strings it
			declined to rewrite. It also highlights the SQL embedded in host-language
			files, as semantic tokens, for editors that layer them over the
			language&rsquo;s own highlighting. Config is re-read for every request, so
			edits to <code>squill.toml</code> apply without a restart.
		</p>
		<p>In Helix, register it and use it for SQL:</p>
		<Code
			lang="toml"
			file="languages.toml"
			code={`[language-server.squill]
command = "squill"
args = ["language-server", "start"]

[[language]]
name = "sql"
language-servers = ["squill"]
auto-format = true`}
		/>
		<p>
			Next to another language&rsquo;s own server, it can contribute just its
			diagnostics about embedded SQL, while that server keeps formatting:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="toml"
			file="languages.toml"
			code={`[[language]]
name = "rust"
language-servers = ["rust-analyzer", { name = "squill", only-features = ["diagnostics"] }]`}
		/>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Neovim</h2>
		<p>
			Neovim 0.11 and later set up language servers without plugins. Register
			squill for SQL and every language it finds SQL in, next to those
			languages&rsquo; own servers:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="lua"
			file="init.lua"
			code={`vim.lsp.config("squill", {
  cmd = { "squill", "language-server", "start" },
  filetypes = {
    "sql", "rust", "go", "python", "javascript", "javascriptreact",
    "typescript", "typescriptreact", "gleam", "cpp", "cs", "java",
    "kotlin", "swift",
  },
  root_markers = { "squill.toml", ".config/squill.toml", ".git" },
})
vim.lsp.enable("squill")`}
		/>
		<p>
			Neovim layers the semantic tokens of every server attached to a buffer, so
			the SQL in a Rust file&rsquo;s strings is highlighted on top of
			rust-analyzer&rsquo;s highlighting. Formatting runs every attached
			server&rsquo;s formatter in turn; to run just squill&rsquo;s, name it:
		</p>
		<Code lang="lua" code={`vim.lsp.buf.format({ name = "squill" })`} />
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Zed</h2>
		<p>
			squill&rsquo;s Zed extension, in{" "}
			<a href="https://github.com/aslilac/squill/tree/trunk/integrations/zed">
				<code>integrations/zed</code>
			</a>
			, runs squill&rsquo;s language server for SQL and the languages it formats
			embedded SQL in; install it with{" "}
			<strong>zed: install dev extension</strong>, and see its README for
			settings. Without it, an external formatter works too.
		</p>
		<p>
			Install the SQL extension for syntax support, then in{" "}
			<code>settings.json</code> set squill as an external formatter.{" "}
			<code>&lbrace;buffer_path&rbrace;</code> passes the buffer&rsquo;s path:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="jsonc"
			file="settings.json"
			code={`{
  "languages": {
    "SQL": {
      "formatter": {
        "external": {
          "command": "squill",
          "arguments": ["fmt", "--stdin-filepath", "{buffer_path}"]
        }
      }
    }
  }
}`}
		/>
		<p>
			Zed runs a list of formatters in order, so for SQL embedded in another
			language, keep its language server&rsquo;s formatting and follow it with
			squill:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="jsonc"
			file="settings.json"
			code={`{
  "languages": {
    "Rust": {
      "formatter": [
        { "language_server": { "name": "rust-analyzer" } },
        {
          "external": {
            "command": "squill",
            "arguments": ["fmt", "--stdin-filepath", "{buffer_path}"]
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}`}
		/>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>VS Code</h2>
		<p>
			squill&rsquo;s VS Code extension runs squill&rsquo;s language server: it
			formats SQL files and the SQL embedded in other languages, and highlights
			the embedded SQL. Build it with{" "}
			<code>
				pnpm install &amp;&amp; pnpm run build &amp;&amp; pnpm run package
			</code>{" "}
			and install the <code>.vsix</code>; its README covers the rest of its
			settings.
		</p>
		<p>
			In <code>settings.json</code>, make squill the SQL formatter, and format
			on save:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="jsonc"
			file="settings.json"
			code={`{
  "[sql]": {
    "editor.defaultFormatter": "aslilac.squill",
    "editor.formatOnSave": true
  }
}`}
		/>
		<p>
			VS Code runs one formatter per file, so in another language that
			language&rsquo;s own formatter keeps the job. squill formats the SQL
			embedded there with a <code>source.formatSql</code> code action instead,
			which runs on save after the formatter. Turn it on for each language whose
			SQL a <code>[[embedded]]</code> rule covers:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="jsonc"
			file="settings.json"
			code={`{
  "[rust]": {
    "editor.codeActionsOnSave": {
      "source.formatSql": "explicit"
    }
  }
}`}
		/>
	</section>
</Docs>
